# Managing the demo-app Service via sudoers

## 1. Purpose
- The `deploy` user is allowed to manage only the `demo-app` service via systemd.
- Commands are executed as `root` without a password prompt.

## 2. Configuration
File: `/etc/sudoers.d/deploy-demo-app`

# Allow deploy to manage only the demo-app service
deploy ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl start demo-app, /usr/bin/systemctl stop demo-app, /usr/bin/systemctl restart demo-app, /usr/bin/systemctl status demo-app

Allowed operations:
- `start` — start the service.
- `stop` — stop the service.
- `restart` — restart the service.
- `status` — view the service status.

Other systemd operations and management of other services are not permitted.

## 3. Configuration and Privilege Verification
- Run as an administrator:
Check the sudoers configuration syntax:
    sudo visudo -c
- List the commands allowed for the user:
    sudo -l -U deploy

## 4. Command Execution Verification
- Switch to the `deploy` user:
    su - deploy
- Test the allowed operations:
    sudo systemctl status demo-app
    sudo systemctl restart demo-app
- Test that management of another service is denied:
    sudo systemctl restart ssh
Expected result: sudo rejects the command because it is not included in the list of permitted commands.

## 5. sudo Log Verification
- View records of commands executed via sudo during the last 10 minutes:
    sudo journalctl _COMM=sudo --since "10 minutes ago"